00:23:03.250 --> 00:23:06.800
an investment of resources in
creativity at a place where

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:10.510
people are paying for it
and we wanna give them value

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:13.330
for it and so that ecosystem works

00:23:13.330 --> 00:23:14.530
from a business standpoint.

00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:17.070
Well stated another way,
every thing that we put out

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:20.530
for free on the internet is
more or less designed to be

00:23:20.530 --> 00:23:25.400
something that a first time
viewer or listener could step

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:28.990
into and get it and appreciate
it and wanna be a part of

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:30.260
what we're doing.
Yeah.

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:34.020
But if you have actually bought into it

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:35.790
and you're like, no I am a Mythical Beast,

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:38.440
this is a part of my life,
I'm gonna support you guys

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:41.290
in what you're doing, well
now we have an avenue for that

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:44.650
and it's really a, well,
those people are willing to

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:49.320
pay for it and we have to
provide a service that's worthy

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:52.140
of being paid for, you know, so it's like,

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:54.550
it's a totally different business model

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:57.360
and so yeah, once you find
that you're creating something

00:23:57.360 --> 00:23:59.990
outside for everybody to
come and watch and it really

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:02.260
makes sense to be something
that's really for a committed

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:07.260
fan, well, that stuff is
moving more and more to be

00:24:07.430 --> 00:24:09.090
a part of the Mythical Society.

00:24:09.090 --> 00:24:09.923
So that means--

00:24:09.923 --> 00:24:14.250
I'm very proud of what
LTAT was and you know,

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:17.820
we weren't critical of
the product as much as

00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:18.890
everything we've already said, it's

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:21.410
kind of a different assessment.

00:24:21.410 --> 00:24:23.420
It was a lot of work for
Stevie, I will say that.

00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:24.928
A lot of work for Stevie.

00:24:24.928 --> 00:24:26.070
Stevie's already--
She did a great job.

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:27.470
Daven did a great job.

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:30.920
As busy as anybody at Mythical but

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:34.230
it ended up being, you know,
it's like she was producing it

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:36.350
and she was hosting it, it ends up being

00:24:36.350 --> 00:24:37.590
a lot of work.
There's a lot, yeah.

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:41.270
It was a lot so once we
started talking about

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:44.770
making that move, like I
said, we didn't have to fill

00:24:44.770 --> 00:24:47.120
Saturdays with anything
but we started to think

00:24:47.970 --> 00:24:50.670
but what if we did, what
would that look like

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:54.530
and I mean, we went through
a number of things like

00:24:56.470 --> 00:24:59.340
the start of ideas but
there was never like

00:25:00.220 --> 00:25:04.010
another idea that we, or two
more ideas that we were like

00:25:04.010 --> 00:25:07.580
really deliberating between,
we really, the brainstorming

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:11.263
process led to the point of us vlogging.

00:25:14.105 --> 00:25:15.450
And I think that came from--

00:25:15.450 --> 00:25:17.530
And then the second decision
which we can talk about,

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:20.060
was like the fact that we
didn't even put it on the

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.139
Good Mythical Morning channel,

00:25:21.139 --> 00:25:22.831
we can talk about that secondarily.

00:25:22.831 --> 00:25:25.510
I think there's a number of things that

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:26.900
kinda led to this point and

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:31.313
for me I kinda see three things.

00:25:32.407 --> 00:25:36.630
I see number one, the decision
to go back to Bowie's Creek

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:39.943
and shoot those three special
episodes for Bleak Creek,

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:44.063
for the Bleak Creek conversations, two,

00:25:48.219 --> 00:25:51.123
I remember the third one and
I just forgot the second one.

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:54.990
The decision to do that, second,

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:57.830
the kind of observing
what's happening on YouTube

00:25:57.830 --> 00:26:01.780
and seeing people do a particular
thing and kinda thinking,

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:06.100
man, we could do that, that
for me that was a motivating

00:26:06.100 --> 00:26:08.350
factor, like seeing people
really connecting with an

00:26:08.350 --> 00:26:10.320
audience in a certain way
and knowing that we would be

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:15.010
good at it just kinda
naturally makes me wanna do it

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:18.530
but then I think the third
point is in the midst of

00:26:18.530 --> 00:26:21.790
trying to get a lot of, you know,

00:26:21.790 --> 00:26:23.430
a lot of people are like,
what do you guys do when

00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:26.190
you're not doing GMM
and I think many of you

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:29.033
now kinda understand, well a lot.

00:26:31.831 --> 00:26:33.460
And one of the things that
we're always trying to do is

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:35.240
we're trying to get these,
what you might call,

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:38.410
traditional projects off
the ground so things that

00:26:38.410 --> 00:26:41.030
require a different model of
financing where you have to

00:26:41.030 --> 00:26:43.460
go pitch something, you
have to talk to people,

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:45.730
you have to develop ideas
with people, things move

00:26:45.730 --> 00:26:48.360
really, really slowly in
that traditional world

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:51.350
where you're used, you know,
we're used to being on YouTube.

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:53.340
It worked really slowly and then

00:26:54.370 --> 00:26:56.330
odds are it'll never happen.

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:57.980
Right.
And no one will ever see it.

00:26:57.980 --> 00:26:59.830
Yeah so you--
But that's part of it.

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:02.170
Yeah and so that's something
we wanna continue to do

00:27:02.170 --> 00:27:04.610
because there are certain
ideas and certain things we

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:07.760
aspire to that can only be
accomplished with that business

00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:11.470
model, some of the biggest and
best ideas we have can only

00:27:11.470 --> 00:27:13.190
be brought to fruition using that

00:27:13.190 --> 00:27:14.518
traditional business model.

00:27:14.518 --> 00:27:16.100
Yeah, I mean, you're talking
about a television series

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:17.640
or multiple, a movie.
A movie, whatever.

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:19.040
I mean--
So we're gonna keep

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:21.590
trying to make those things
happen and they will happen

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:24.210
but because they can be
frustratingly slow and like Link

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:28.290
said, they can have small
chances of actually happening,

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:30.770
we kinda just get creatively antsy.

00:27:30.770 --> 00:27:31.990
Yeah.
And I think that

00:27:31.990 --> 00:27:36.990
this decision to do these
vlogs is really an answer to,

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:41.380
one, to try something new
and invigorating creatively,

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:43.630
something that we can be excited about.

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:45.200
Yeah so to kinda go back through those,

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:46.500
I think for the first one,

00:27:47.594 --> 00:27:52.594
we replayed the experience
in the assessment of when

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:54.540
we went back to Bowie's
Creek and shot that like,

00:27:54.540 --> 00:27:56.900
docu-follow stuff, like
basically making our own

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:58.790
mini-documentary and
deciding to put it within

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:02.990
Good Mythical Morning and
it was such a fun experience

00:28:02.990 --> 00:28:05.553
to be in that mode
again where it was like,

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:09.790
we're out of the studio,
we're being ourselves,

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:12.927
we're excited about something,
about doing something

00:28:12.927 --> 00:28:16.300
and it's being documented and
we just had this faith that,

00:28:16.300 --> 00:28:19.040
okay, without trying too hard

00:28:20.670 --> 00:28:23.770
to make it into something,
let's just honestly pursue

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:27.220
this goal and have this hope
that that will just come

00:28:27.220 --> 00:28:29.560
across in the edit and
that people will enjoy it

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:30.770
and you did and that really happened

00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:32.270
and it was really encouraging.

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:37.580
So yeah I think it did spark that creative

00:28:38.610 --> 00:28:40.700
itch to do that again.

00:28:40.700 --> 00:28:43.688
And if you go look at
some of the comments,

00:28:43.688 --> 00:28:47.580
this wasn't necessarily, we
already thinking this but

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:50.900
if you look at some of the
comments on those videos

00:28:50.900 --> 00:28:54.500
you will see people say things
like, I would love to see

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:59.150
you guys do like a vlog of your lives

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:01.130
and so it's just like,
okay people are making the

00:29:01.130 --> 00:29:03.790
connection that this feels
like that kind of content

00:29:03.790 --> 00:29:06.010
which has become a genre in and of itself.

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:10.380
Right, I think the other point and again

00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:12.700
we talked about it at the end of the year

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:15.600
with our top 10s 'cause
I was talking about

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:17.550
the birthday, your birthday day.

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:18.520
Yeah, yeah.
And there's,

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:21.940
there's two aspects of that, there's like,

00:29:21.940 --> 00:29:25.720
there's the cultivating
our friendship and sharing

00:29:25.720 --> 00:29:29.310
that experience which I think
I'd like to come back to that

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:33.570
but then just the other aspect
of, we were just filming

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:37.150
Instagram stories and it
was a lot of fun so like,

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:39.780
I think as opposed to the
Bowie's Creek documentary

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:42.760
when we were just filming
ourselves on our phones and there

00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:45.480
was like nobody else
there, it kinda made it

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:49.482
even more exciting
because it was all on us.

00:29:49.482 --> 00:29:50.315
Yeah.

00:29:50.315 --> 00:29:54.610
And it was the most honest
version of ourselves

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:56.560
when we were doing
everything, just documenting

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:57.910
what we were doing so I think we

00:29:57.910 --> 00:30:01.240
very quickly got to, let's film ourselves.

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:02.600
Yeah.

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:04.278
Let's film each other.

00:30:04.278 --> 00:30:07.077
We were tossing around an idea.

00:30:07.077 --> 00:30:10.040
We were driving somewhere
and we started talking about

00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:14.460
what if we, this was before
we came up with this idea,

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:15.980
we were talking about
doing like a little mini

00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:19.490
scripted thing where we play
characters who are filming

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:21.940
themselves and each other on
their phones but it's very

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:25.530
character based and like it
would be totally scripted.

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:28.880
We got excited about
that on that one drive.

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:30.110
Maybe we'll do that in the future.

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:30.943
We might do that.
Maybe that'd be

00:30:30.943 --> 00:30:32.900
something on Instagram, I
mean I don't know, we're not

00:30:32.900 --> 00:30:36.010
actively pursuing that
right now but whenever we're

00:30:36.010 --> 00:30:38.980
driving somewhere which we
rarely do but if you gotta

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:41.470
drive across town, we end up
talking about things like this

00:30:41.470 --> 00:30:43.790
where we'll hatch an
idea and then we'll just

00:30:43.790 --> 00:30:46.010
put it on the shelf so
maybe it'll come back down

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:48.840
off the shelf but I do
think when we revisited

00:30:49.960 --> 00:30:53.200
the brainstorming that
led to this, we were kinda

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:57.110
sideways accessing the feeling,
the excitement from that

00:30:57.110 --> 00:30:59.540
idea in saying let's film ourselves,

00:30:59.540 --> 00:31:02.714
let's just keep it as small as possible.

00:31:02.714 --> 00:31:05.570
Also, I think that's a reaction to

00:31:05.570 --> 00:31:07.670
and again, it's not a negative
reaction to the fact that

00:31:07.670 --> 00:31:12.480
we have a big team that every, you know,

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:15.000
specializes in so many different
things and does so many

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:18.430
things much better than we
can and works within a system

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:22.210
that's been created to
produce a bunch of content

00:31:22.210 --> 00:31:23.563
to get it all out there.

00:31:24.950 --> 00:31:29.340
It's exciting to just kinda
go back to our roots so

00:31:29.340 --> 00:31:31.070
it's not saying anything
negative about the team.

00:31:31.070 --> 00:31:34.450
All that's positive and the
fact that we can brainstorm

00:31:34.450 --> 00:31:35.530
these ideas where we can go off

00:31:35.530 --> 00:31:37.090
and shoot something ourselves

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:42.840
is kind of a, it's to their
credit but it's exciting to do

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:44.950
something that was polar
opposite from that.

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:48.990
Well I think more specifically,
it's more in line with

00:31:48.990 --> 00:31:52.820
the spirit of the internet, so, you know--

00:31:52.820 --> 00:31:54.540
So to your second point.

00:31:54.540 --> 00:31:56.900
Yeah, well and also when you think about

00:31:56.900 --> 00:31:58.000
Good Mythical Morning,

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:04.790
you know, years ago, once
we started making GMM,

00:32:04.790 --> 00:32:07.863
our assumption was as soon
as GMM started working,

00:32:08.790 --> 00:32:12.360
what we thought was
going to happen is that

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:15.730
all these people were gonna
just, like, we're gonna start

00:32:15.730 --> 00:32:20.320
doing what we do which is then
we're gonna make a sort of

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:24.760
produced daily show that kinda feels like

00:32:24.760 --> 00:32:27.120
the internet's answer to a talk show.

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:29.690
Now, yes some people tried it

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:34.290
and there are some
iterations of it out there

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:39.420
but I think what most people
quickly realize is that,

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:44.420
man, this is really hard,
it's really, really hard to do

00:32:44.750 --> 00:32:47.330
and do it for a long period
of time and to do it well

00:32:47.330 --> 00:32:49.990
for a long period of time and
the only reason we can do it

00:32:49.990 --> 00:32:52.300
is because of the team that
we've built around us that now,

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:56.080
like you said, makes it
where it's a possibility but

00:32:57.050 --> 00:32:59.870
one of the things that comes
along with really efficient

00:32:59.870 --> 00:33:03.850
processes being in place
for creating things like

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:08.090
Good Mythical Morning is that, you know,

00:33:08.090 --> 00:33:11.830
they're going to end up
having a little bit more of a

00:33:11.830 --> 00:33:16.560
polished and refined
quality to them like, GMM

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:19.530
kinda feels like, like I said, YouTube's

00:33:19.530 --> 00:33:21.523
answer to a television show, right?

00:33:23.054 --> 00:33:25.270
And it is what it is and it
really works and it's worked

00:33:25.270 --> 00:33:28.230
for a long time and it is
really, it's, you know,

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:31.930
has large, like, the heart of
GMM is still very much just

00:33:31.930 --> 00:33:33.350
as just being friends together and

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:34.610
interacting with something.

00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:35.443
Right.

00:33:35.443 --> 00:33:39.500
But it doesn't have this DIY
feel to it because we had

00:33:39.500 --> 00:33:43.830
to lose the DIY feel in
order to do anything else.

00:33:43.830 --> 00:33:47.300
So now it has this, like,
oh a studio makes this show.

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:49.710
This show's coming from a group of people.

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:52.980
Yeah, those guys who host it
seem like they may be in charge

00:33:52.980 --> 00:33:54.980
or they created it or whatever but

00:33:54.980 --> 00:33:57.130
it has this group effort feel to it

00:33:58.370 --> 00:34:01.190
and I think that one of the things that,

00:34:01.190 --> 00:34:03.440
there's a part of it that people--

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:06.490
If it didn't it would never be, it would,

00:34:06.490 --> 00:34:07.570
we would've stopped years ago.

00:34:07.570 --> 00:34:08.830
Yeah that's what I'm trying to say.

00:34:08.830 --> 00:34:11.690
If it didn't have that feel
to it, that group effort feel,

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:14.660
it would not exist so that is what it is

00:34:14.660 --> 00:34:17.890
but at the same time, there's
this thing that's been

00:34:17.890 --> 00:34:21.320
happening, that's been happening
since the onset of YouTube

00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:24.980
but has kinda taken on a new
life and it is people who are

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:28.030
kinda documenting themselves,
now a lot of people are doing

00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:30.450
it like, if you take a look at
David Dobrick, who's probably

00:34:30.450 --> 00:34:33.880
the most successful example
of this, this is a guy living

00:34:33.880 --> 00:34:37.840
an incredible life and
documenting it constantly and then

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:41.090
putting together the best four
minutes and 20 seconds every

00:34:41.090 --> 00:34:43.480
week, I guess or however often he does it.

00:34:43.480 --> 00:34:45.020
I think so, yeah.

00:34:45.020 --> 00:34:48.140
And you're along for the
ride and I think that,

00:34:48.140 --> 00:34:50.210
you know, it ends up kinda
feeding on itself in a way.

00:34:50.210 --> 00:34:53.390
It's just like, now
that my life is my show,

00:34:53.390 --> 00:34:56.150
my life is going to be
produced in a way that's gonna

00:34:56.150 --> 00:34:58.430
make an interesting show
and it becomes this thing

00:34:58.430 --> 00:35:01.510
that like, he's sort of
the prime example of like,

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:03.940
that genre really working
in building an audience.

00:35:03.940 --> 00:35:06.580
Now that is not what we
are going to be doing.

00:35:06.580 --> 00:35:07.910
Right.
Because there's also

00:35:07.910 --> 00:35:12.350
another version and I'd say
if we're drawing inspiration,

00:35:12.350 --> 00:35:16.710
it's more from this other
version that is more isolated.

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:19.460
We're not constantly gonna
be filming ourselves,

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:22.790
much less our families
and our personal lives.

00:35:22.790 --> 00:35:24.210
Yeah.

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:29.210
But our idea is to have
specific ideas for,

00:35:31.920 --> 00:35:33.810
I guess I'll call it an episode.

00:35:33.810 --> 00:35:36.440
I'll call it a video, it's not an episode.

00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:38.720
We'll have a particular idea
for a video and then we'll

00:35:38.720 --> 00:35:42.240
execute that idea, we'll film
it ourselves and then we'll

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:45.830
get, we'll bring it here and
it will be edited with our,

00:35:45.830 --> 00:35:50.793
under ours and the proper
team member's supervision.

00:35:53.213 --> 00:35:55.860
So it's more like a Jenna Marbles video.

00:35:55.860 --> 00:35:58.530
I mean all of her videos at
her house, I'm not saying all

00:35:58.530 --> 00:36:01.320
of our videos are gonna
be at one of our homes,

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:05.280
nothing's gonna be on the
set and it could very well be

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:07.060
at our homes or out and about but it's--

00:36:07.060 --> 00:36:07.893
Right.

00:36:07.893 --> 00:36:09.547
It's whatever the idea that
we're excited about doing--

00:36:09.547 --> 00:36:10.800
